.TH TAIL 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
tail \- print the last part of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.RB [ \-fFqv ]
.RB [ \-c
.RI [+] count ]
.RB [ \-n
.RI [+] count ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR seconds ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B tail
.BI \- count
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B tail
.BI + count
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tail
writes the last part of each
.I file
to standard output: by default the last 10 lines.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
This
.B tail
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
A
.I file
that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped, and the
remaining files are still written.
When none can be opened,
.B tail
reports
.B no files
and exits.
.SS Counts
A count is a decimal number, optionally followed by one of the suffixes
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
or
.BR m ,
which multiply it by 512, 1024, or 1048576.
Suffixes are lowercase.
The result must be from 0 to 4294967295.
Hexadecimal and white space are not accepted.
A count that is malformed or out of range is an error, reported before
any file is read.
.PP
A count may begin with one
.BR \- ,
which changes nothing, so
.B \-n \-5
is the same as
.BR "\-n 5" .
A count that begins with
.B +
is a starting position instead of a length:
.B \-n +5
writes everything from the 5th line on, and
.B \-c +5
everything from the 5th byte on.
A count of
.B +0
or
.B +1
writes the whole input.
.PP
With
.BR \-n ,
.B tail
counts lines, which end at an LF.
A CR is an ordinary byte.
A last line without an LF counts as a line, and is written as it is,
without adding one.
With
.BR \-c ,
it counts bytes.
A count of 0 writes nothing.
.PP
If an option is given more than once, the last one is used.
If both
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
are given, in either order,
.B tail
counts bytes, using the count of
.BR \-n ,
and a leading
.B +
on either count selects counting from the start.
.SS Headers
With more than one
.I file
operand that could be opened, or with
.BR \-v ,
.B tail
writes a header before the output of each file: a line of the form
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
==> name <==
.EE
.RE
.PP
where
.I name
is the operand as given, or
.B standard input
for standard input when there are no operands.
Each header after the first starts with an LF.
That gives an empty line between files, or ends the last line of a file
that had no final LF.
A file that could not be opened does not count, so
.B tail \-n 1 a.txt missing.txt
writes no header.
With
.BR \-F ,
a file that does not exist yet counts.
.B \-q
turns headers off.
.SS Following
With
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F ,
.B tail
writes the initial output, and then runs until interrupted.
Every
.B \-s
seconds it reads each file again, in operand order, and writes any data
added since the last read.
When headers are on, it first writes a header, which starts with an LF,
whenever the data comes from a different file than the data it wrote last.
After the initial output, the last operand counts as the file written
last, even when it did not exist yet.
.PP
If at a poll a file is not empty but is shorter than the position
.B tail
has reached in it,
.B tail
reads the file again from its start, with no message.
.PP
.B \-F
also retries by name.
A file that could not be opened at the start is reported, but does not
change the exit status.
At each poll,
.B tail
checks whether the name still refers to the file that it has open, by
volume serial number and file index, and if not, opens the name again.
It reports on standard error
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
tail: \fIname\fR has appeared; following end of new file
tail: \fIname\fR has been replaced; following end of new file
tail: \fIname\fR has been renamed or deleted: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
for a name that now opens but did not before, a name that now refers to
a different file, and a name that no longer opens.
Despite the wording, a file that has appeared or been replaced is read
from its start.
A replaced file is read only after the open file runs out of data.
After a name is reported renamed or deleted,
.B tail
keeps following the file it has open, and repeats the message at each
poll.
Standard input has no name to check, so
.B \-F
reports it as renamed or deleted at each poll.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-qn5 ,
and an option's argument may be attached or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
Options may also follow operands, as in
.BR "tail f.txt \-n 5" ,
unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set, when the first operand ends them.
A lone
.B \-
is an operand.
.TP
.BI "\-c " count
Write the last
.I count
bytes instead of lines.
With a leading
.BR + ,
write everything from byte
.I count
on.
See
.BR Counts .
.TP
.BI "\-n " count
Write the last
.I count
lines.
The default is 10.
With a leading
.BR + ,
write everything from line
.I count
on.
.TP
.B \-q
Never write headers.
.TP
.B \-v
Always write headers, even for one operand or for standard input with no
operands.
This wins over
.B \-q
whatever the order.
.TP
.B \-f
Follow the files as they grow.
See
.BR Following .
.TP
.B \-F
Like
.BR \-f ,
but also retry by name.
.TP
.BI "\-s " seconds
Wait
.I seconds
between polls with
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F .
The default is 1.
The value is a decimal integer from 0 to 2147483647, without a fraction
or suffix.
A value of 0 polls continuously.
A value above 4294967 wraps around: the wait is the value times 1000,
modulo 4294967296, in milliseconds.
Without
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F ,
the value is checked and then ignored.
.TP
\fB\-\fIcount\fR, \fB+\fIcount\fR
As the first argument only,
.BI \- count
is the same as
.BI "\-n " count
and
.BI + count
is the same as
.BI "\-n +" count ,
except that the first character of
.I count
must be a digit.
Other options may follow, as in
.BR "tail \-5 \-q a.txt b.txt" ,
but the digits cannot be grouped with them:
.B \-5f
is an error.
A later
.B \-c
or
.B \-n
replaces the number, but not the
.B +
of
.BI + count ,
so
.B tail +2 \-n 3
writes everything from line 3 on.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
Other invalid options print the usage on standard error and exit with
status 1.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted, as described in
.BR NOTES ,
and the console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
Read by the C library's option parser.
When it is set, option parsing stops at the first operand.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or count, a
.I file
that could not be opened (except with
.BR \-F ),
a read error, or a write error.
With
.B \-c
on a seekable file, a read error, or a write error other than a closed
pipe, is reported but does not change the status.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.PP
With
.B \-f
or
.BR \-F ,
.B tail
runs until it is interrupted or an error ends it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write the last 20 lines of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-n 20 build.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the last line of each of two files, with headers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-n 1 a.txt b.txt
==> a.txt <==
last line of a

==> b.txt <==
last line of b
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the last 3 lines of every log, without headers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-qn3 *.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Skip the first line of a file, such as the header of a CSV file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-n +2 data.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the last 4 KiB of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-c 4k server.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the end of a command's output:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ make 2>&1 | tail \-n 30
.EE
.RE
.PP
Watch a log grow, until interrupted with Ctrl+C:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-f server.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Wait for a log that does not exist yet, then follow it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-F build/output.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the last 3 lines in the traditional way:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ tail \-3 notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR tail :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
no
.BR \-\-version ,
and no
.BR \-z .
.IP \(bu 2
Following polls once per
.B \-s
interval, in whole seconds.
.IP \(bu 2
Counts are decimal with the lowercase suffixes
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
and
.B m
only.
GNU suffixes such as
.BR K ,
.BR kB ,
and
.BR MB
are errors.
.IP \(bu 2
Counts are limited to 4294967295, in the 64-bit kit too.
.IP \(bu 2
Where GNU
.B tail
ignores
.B \-f
for a pipe on standard input, this one keeps polling the pipe forever,
even after the writer exits.
Interrupt it.
.IP \(bu 2
When both
.B \-c
and
.B \-n
are given, GNU
.B tail
uses the last one.
This one counts bytes, with the count of
.BR \-n .
.PP
On input that can seek, such as a regular file,
.B tail
with
.B \-c
reads only the bytes it writes.
With
.B \-n
it starts reading about 64 KiB before the end for each requested line,
but at least 960 KiB and at most 2 GiB before the end, so that it does
not read all of a very large file.
That assumes the last lines are shorter than that on average.
If they are longer, it writes fewer than
.I count
lines, and the first may be partial.
A count that begins with
.B +
reads from the start of the input.
.PP
On input that cannot seek, such as a pipe,
.B tail
reads all of the input.
It holds the last
.I count
lines in memory, or with
.B \-c
allocates
.I count
bytes in advance.
.PP
Very large counts without a leading
.B +
fail.
A
.B \-n
count of 2147483648 or more, or a
.B \-c
count of 4294966784 or more on input that cannot seek, crashes
.B tail
when the input is not empty.
In the 64-bit kit, a smaller
.B \-c
count of 2147483136 or more on input that cannot seek fails with
.BR "read error: Invalid argument" .
.PP
Files and standard input and output are binary, so CR LF line endings
are copied unchanged, and lines end only at LF.
.PP
File names may use drive letters, backslashes, and forward slashes, and
a header shows the name as given.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/tty
(the console), and
.I /dev/fd/N
work as on Unix.
A directory operand fails with
.BR "Permission denied" .
.PP
While
.B tail
has a file open, including each file that it follows, Windows refuses to
delete or rename the file with a sharing violation, which busybox
.B rm
and
.B mv
report as
.BR "Permission denied" .
Other programs can still append to the file or truncate it.
A log cannot be rotated by renaming it while
.B tail
follows it, and
.B \-F
differs from
.B \-f
in practice for a file that does not exist yet.
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, to the console's code page, unless
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
or
.B LANG
is
.BR C .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR less (1)
